\section{The Shape That Keeps Returning}

Start with the engineering fact. A transformer does not ``understand'' sentences one by one. It dissolves them. Each token is mapped into a vector, and then, through stacked layers of attention and non-linearity, the model comes to represent whole phrases, paragraphs, genres as points in a high-dimensional space. Eight thousand numbers, say, for a single sentence. Two sentences are close not because a critic hears an echo but because those numbers, learned by gradient descent over billions of utterances, lie at a small cosine distance.

Meaning has an address now.

Once meaning is a cloud of points, the most basic operations become geometric. Removing a corpus is deleting a subcloud. Adding specialised data is thickening some regions, hollowing others. Fine-tuning is a slight turn of the whole geometry. RLHF is more radical: for each point, we learn not only where it sits but how much the system should want to go there. A reward model draws an invisible field over the manifold. Some directions become downhill, others uphill. A trajectory through this space --- what we earlier called an Evolving Text (ET) --- is a line feeling its way across a landscape of ridges and basins.

Nothing in the architecture demands that this landscape be shaped to minimise user discomfort, or maximise ``helpfulness,'' or avoid certain political stances. The manifold is open. From almost any starting point, there are many long, coherent trajectories that could be taken. What narrows that openness into a channel is not tensor algebra. It is a set of decisions about what should and should not be easy to say.

The decisions are structural: which texts enter pre-training, which continuations human annotators are told to reward, which refusal patterns are installed as safety rails, which trajectories through meaning-space are declared off-limits and subjected to high alignment tax. They sit at the joints of the system, where geometry meets governance.

Yuk Hui gives this recurrent structure a name: \emph{cosmotechnics}.\cite{Hui2016} A cosmotechnics is a particular way of welding together a view of the cosmos with a repertoire of technics. There is no world of neutral ``technology'' to which cultures add ornament. There are only different welds: different answers to what exists, what matters, who counts, realised in tools and infrastructures.

Alignment, in this sense, is cosmotechnics in action. It is where one civilisation's picture of order is poured into the manifold of meaning and naturalised as if it were structure itself.


\section{Hui's Cut Through ``Technology''}

Hui's gesture begins with a split.\cite{Hui2016} The word \emph{technology} pretends to name a universal species. A steam engine in Manchester, an irrigation system in the Yellow River basin, a smartphone factory in Shenzhen: all become stages of the same story, variations on one ladder of \emph{technological development} climbing toward a single apex.

Cosmotechnics cuts across this fiction. In classical Chinese thought, for example, \emph{dao} and \emph{qi} never hardened into separate domains of moral philosophy and engineering. \emph{Dao} is not only a teaching about right conduct; it is the patterned flow of heaven, earth, and society. \emph{Qi} is not only a neutral ``material substrate''; it is the concrete configuration of forces in which that flow is realised. Scholar-officials were trained in both ritual and hydraulics. City walls, canals, and bureaucratic procedures were all ways of aligning with the same cosmic order.

An indigenous Australian songline is likewise not ``culture'' painted on top of a neutral technique of wayfinding. The song is the track. It encodes law, genealogy, story, and navigation in one medium. The question ``Is this technology or belief?'' misfires; the weld is older than the split that made such questions seem natural.

In each case, cosmos and techn\={e} are co-specified. How the world hangs together and how tools should behave are learned together. Those bonds are enforced in institutions: curricula, rituals, embodied practices. They sediment in infrastructures. They persist over the \emph{longue dur\'ee}: the slow, deep time of civilisational habits.

Modern industrial AI comes from a different weld. Its cosmology is recognisable. Matter is value-neutral. Reason is abstract and universal. Individuals are interior points of consciousness bounded by skin, owners of rights and preferences. Tools are external extensions: means to ends defined elsewhere. Morality is keeping those means from harming those ends.

The alignment stack is where that weld bites into neural weights and tokens. Corpus filters, reward rubrics, safety classifications, refusal scripts: all instantiate decisions about who the world is for and how speech is allowed to circulate. They are not post-hoc ethics draped over neutral structure. They are the way one cosmology governs a manifold whose reach now extends through language itself.


\section{Geometry Under Rule}

The manifold is a medium of rule. Braudel's triptych of time maps where, and for how long, that rule bites.\cite{mtreview-rlhf-labor}

At the deepest register, the \emph{longue dur\'ee}, we have pre-training. Somewhere, a list of sources is written: major newspapers, Wikipedia, academic journals, code repositories, forums, books, scraped web pages. Somewhere else, a list is written again, this time of what is excluded as ``toxic,'' ``low quality,'' or legally risky. These lists are not published in full. They act on months of GPU time. Their decisions fade from view, but their effect is durable. They determine which ways of speaking even appear as points.

Above that lies the \emph{conjoncture} of fine-tuning and RLHF. Here, the manifold, once carved, is pushed and pulled by shorter runs of training. Instruction-following datasets teach the model how to behave in a more task-oriented way. Human annotators, following detailed guidelines, rank outputs: this one is ``helpful,'' that one ``unsafe,'' a third ``disallowed.'' Reward models learn to imitate these rankings.\cite{christiano2017deep,bai2022constitutional} The surface of the manifold deforms. Basins deepen around some styles of response; cliffs rise around others.

At the fastest register, the \emph{\'ev\'enement}, lie system prompts, safety filters, and per-conversation memories. These are small pieces of text and parameter knobs with enormous leverage. A forty-line system prompt instructs a model to speak as a legal assistant, a therapist, a Solidity auditor. A safety classifier flags a continuation as too graphic and forces a rewrite. A memory component recalls that ``earlier you said you were anxious'' and pulls the trajectory back toward reassurance.

These layers interact. The Bible experiments we described earlier made this visible as data. When we embedded verses and then queried the model with fragments of contemporary speech, the returns clustered. Psalms drew in 308 of them; Job, Isaiah, and the Gospels sat in neighbouring wells. Centuries of commentary had been compacted into a geometry with measurable basins. If RLHF were to penalise lament or imprecatory language as ``negative,'' it would not only alter current outputs; it would, in effect, pull those wells shallower for future trajectories.

